{
  "subtype": "what_if",
  "outcome_variable": "travel_expense_declined_status",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "expense_category",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "The query is specifically about travel expenses; expense_category identifies which records are travel vs. other, effectively a scoping/subsetting attribute already implied by the query. It is not an intervenable lever for reducing declines."
    },
    {
      "name": "entry_method",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "How an expense is entered (manual/automated/self_input) is a configurable, settable process property an actor could change, and it plausibly affects whether the expense is declined (e.g., manual entry causing errors). This is a valid intervenable lever."
    },
    {
      "name": "asset_action_type",
      "causal_role": "other",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Asset action types (registration, procurement, deployment) pertain to hardware asset records, not travel expenses. Largely irrelevant to the travel-expense outcome."
    },
    {
      "name": "has_source_id",
      "causal_role": "other",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "A generic data-completeness flag. Could be a mediator of data-quality issues but is not clearly a common cause of a settable treatment and the outcome; treated as ambiguous/generic."
    },
    {
      "name": "ci_brand",
      "causal_role": "other",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Vendor/brand of the configuration item; mostly hardware-oriented and not an intervenable lever for reducing travel-expense declines, nor a clear confounder of entry_method."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["entry_method"],
  "treatment_intervenable": true,
  "confounders": [
    {
      "name": "expense_category",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "plausibility": 0.3
    }
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.1,
  "rationale": "One plausibly intervenable treatment is present (entry_method: a settable process property that could affect decline rates). However, the columns essentially fail the confounding test. A valid what-if requires common causes of BOTH the treatment (entry_method) and the outcome (decline status) that are NOT already named in the query. The only confounder-like column, expense_category, is scoping already implied by the query, and its causal link to entry_method is weak. The other added columns (asset_action_type, ci_brand, has_source_id) are hardware/asset-oriented and largely irrelevant to travel-expense declines — they do not function as confounders adjusting the entry_method→decline effect. No unnamed, high-plausibility confounder is supplied, so the interventional identification claim is not realised."
}

